---
import Band from '../band/Band.astro'
import SectionHead from '../band/SectionHead.astro'
import FaqItem from '../marketing/FaqItem.astro'

const DOCS = 'https://docs.dumdummisha.ai'
---

<Band id="faq" width={820}>
  <SectionHead eyebrow="FAQ" title="Questions, answers first." />
  <div class="dd-faq">
    <FaqItem question="Do I need a Slack app or admin approval?" isOpen>
      No Slack app, so there's nothing for a workspace admin to approve. A browser extension lets me read and post in
      the Slack tab you're already signed in to, with your own session. Slack's terms and your workplace's rules for
      tools and AI still apply. <a href={`${DOCS}/understand/how-it-works#without-a-slack-app`}>How that works</a>.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Where do my messages and code go?">
      I run on your computer, and there is no Dumdum Misha server, so your work never reaches the person who made me.
      The messages, code and tool results I work with go to Anthropic under your own account, the same as when you use
      Claude Code yourself, and to OpenAI only if you add GPT workers. <a href={`${DOCS}/privacy`}>The privacy policy</a>
      lists every recipient.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Are you going to replace me?">
      No. I'm dumber than you on purpose. I take the routine part and bring you the decisions I can't safely settle.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Are your replies marked?">
      By default, yes: I post through your Slack account, and my replies start with my emoji and are in italics, so
      colleagues can tell. Stealth mode drops both if you'd rather the marker didn't show.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="What can my colleagues ask you?">
      Questions about a project's code in that project's channels, and routine requests. Only people you allow can ask
      me for changes. I never share credentials or private messages.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="What can you merge or deploy?">
      I merge only my own pull requests, and only in repositories you enable. I review a colleague's pull request but
      never merge or rewrite it. Deploys stop at dev; staging and production stay yours.
      <a href={`${DOCS}/reference/permissions`}>Every permission, in detail</a>.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Can someone trick you into breaking your rules?">
      I've been live in a real workplace Slack, where tens of people tried to talk me out of my rules. That doesn't make
      me trick-proof, so the decisions that matter come to you, and only people you allow can ask me for changes.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Which AI do you use?">
      Today, Claude, through your own unmodified Claude Code on your Claude subscription. Bigger jobs go to workers in
      Claude Code or OpenCode. Add GPT and a second provider reviews every ticket and change; without it, a fresh
      Claude session does the reviewing. <a href={`${DOCS}/understand/how-it-works`}>How it fits together</a>.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="What do you cost?">
      Nothing for now. You pay for your own Claude subscription and, if you add GPT, your own OpenAI one.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Is this only for developers?">
      For now, mostly. The pilot starts with software teams, but catch-ups, tickets and plain answers about the code
      don't need you to write any. The aim is a broader personal assistant.
    </FaqItem>
    <FaqItem question="Who is behind Dumdum Misha?">
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/michaljarnot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >Michal Jarnot<span class="dd-vh"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
      >, a software engineer with 10+ years of experience building products for Silicon Valley companies. He built me
      because he needed one.
    </FaqItem>
  </div>
</Band>
